turn6494view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.15446v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 162\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 17 Dec 2025]\nL9: # Title:Toward expert-level motivational interviewing for health behavior improvement with LLMs\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Run-ze Hu , cite6†Yang Yang , cite6†Yi-hang Yang , cite7†Jing-qi Kong , cite8†Jia-hui Luo , cite9†Wen-yu Yang , cite10†Jing Chen , cite11†Jing-yao Liu , cite12†Hui-qun Zeng , cite13†Lei Zhang , cite14†Zheng Liu L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Toward expert-level motivational interviewing for health behavior improvement with LLMs, by Run-ze Hu and 10 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite15†View PDF L16: > Abstract:Background: Motivational interviewing (MI) is an effective counseling approach for promoting health behavior change, but its impact is constrained by the need for highly trained human counselors. Objective: This study aimed to explore a scalable alternative by developing and evaluating Large Language Models for Motivational Interviewing (MI-LLMs).\nL17: Methods: We first curated five Chinese psychological counseling corpora and, using GPT-4 with an MI-informed prompt, transcribed multi-turn dialogues from the two highest-quality datasets (CPsyCounD and PsyDTCorpus) into 2,040 MI-style counseling conversations, of which 2,000 were used for training and 40 for testing. Three Chinese-capable open-source LLMs (Baichuan2-7B-Chat, ChatGLM-4-9B-Chat and Llama-3-8B-Chinese-Chat-v2) were fine-tuned on this corpus and were named as MI-LLMs.\nL18: We evaluated MI-LLMs using round-based automatic metrics and expert manual coding with the Motivational Interviewing Treatment Integrity (MITI) Coding Manual 4.2.1.\nL19: Results: Across all three models, fine-tuning substantially improved BLEU-4 and ROUGE scores compared with the base models, and manual coding showed that MI-LLMs achieved technical and relational global scores, and MI-adherent ratios that approached those of real MI dialogues, although complex reflections and reflection-to-question ratios remained less frequent.\nL20: Conclusions: These findings provide initial evidence that MI-oriented fine-tuning can endow general-purpose LLMs with core MI-consistent counseling behaviors, suggesting a scalable pathway toward AI-assisted health behavior change support while underscoring the need for further work on data scale, complex MI skills and real-world intervention trials.\nL21: Comments:  | 26 pages, 3 figures\nL22: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL23: Cite as:  | cite16†arXiv:2512.15446 [cs.CL]\nL24:    | (or cite17†arXiv:2512.15446v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL25:    | cite18†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2512.15446†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL26: Journal reference:  | JMIR Form Res. 2026;10:e89077\nL27: Related DOI (Digital Object Identifier):  | cite19†https://doi.org/10.2196/89077†doi.org DOI(s) linking to related resources\nL28: ## Submission history\nL29: \nL30: From: Runze Hu [cite20†view email ]\nL31: [v1] Wed, 17 Dec 2025 13:43:26 UTC (1,613 KB)\nL32: \nL33: Full-text links:\nL34: \nL35: ## Access Paper:\nL36: \nL37: View a PDF of the paper titled Toward expert-level motivational interviewing for health behavior improvement with LLMs, by Run-ze Hu and 10 other authors\nL38: \nL39:   * cite15†View PDF L40: \nL41: cite21†view license†creativecommons.org L42: \nL43: ### Current browse context:\nL44: \nL45: cs.CL\nL46: \nL47: cite22†< prev |   cite23†next > L48: \nL49: cite24†new | cite25†recent | cite26†2025-12 L50: \nL51: Change to bro