ic Discovery (https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.15567v1)\nciteturn6494view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.15567v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 167\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 17 Dec 2025 (this version), latest version 8 May 2026 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:Evaluating Large Language Models in Scientific Discovery\nL10: Authors:cite6†Zhangde Song , cite7†Jieyu Lu , cite8†Yuanqi Du , cite9†Botao Yu , cite10†Thomas M. Pruyn , cite11†Yue Huang , cite12†Kehan Guo , cite13†Xiuzhe Luo , cite14†Yuanhao Qu , cite14†Yi Qu , cite15†Yinkai Wang , cite16†Haorui Wang , cite17†Jeff Guo , cite18†Jingru Gan , cite19†Parshin Shojaee , cite20†Di Luo , cite21†Andres M Bran , cite22†Gen Li , cite23†Qiyuan Zhao , cite24†Shao-Xiong Lennon Luo , cite25†Yuxuan Zhang , cite26†Xiang Zou , cite27†Wanru Zhao , cite28†Yifan F.\nL11: Zhang , cite29†Wucheng Zhang , cite30†Shunan Zheng , cite31†Saiyang Zhang , cite32†Sartaaj Takrim Khan , cite33†Mahyar Rajabi-Kochi , cite34†Samantha Paradi-Maropakis , cite35†Tony Baltoiu , cite36†Fengyu Xie , cite37†Tianyang Chen , cite38†Kexin Huang , cite39†Weiliang Luo , cite40†Meijing Fang , cite41†Xin Yang , cite42†Lixue Cheng , cite43†Jiajun He , cite44†Soha Hassoun , cite45†Xiangliang Zhang , cite46†Wei Wang , cite47†Chandan K. Reddy , cite48†Chao Zhang , cite49†Zhiling Zheng , cite50†Mengdi Wang , cite51†Le Cong , cite52†Carla P.\nL12: Gomes , cite53†Chang-Yu Hsieh , cite54†Aditya Nandy , cite55†Philippe Schwaller , cite56†Heather J. Kulik , cite57†Haojun Jia , cite58†Huan Sun , cite59†Seyed Mohamad Moosavi , cite60†Chenru Duan L13: View a PDF of the paper titled Evaluating Large Language Models in Scientific Discovery, by Zhangde Song and 55 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite61†View PDF cite62†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly applied to scientific research, yet prevailing science benchmarks probe decontextualized knowledge and overlook the iterative reasoning, hypothesis generation, and observation interpretation that drive scientific discovery.\nL17: We introduce a scenario-grounded benchmark that evaluates LLMs across biology, chemistry, materials, and physics, where domain experts define research projects of genuine interest and decompose them into modular research scenarios from which vetted questions are sampled. The framework assesses models at two levels: (i) question-level accuracy on scenario-tied items and (ii) project-level performance, where models must propose testable hypotheses, design simulations or experiments, and interpret results.\nL18: Applying this two-phase scientific discovery evaluation (SDE) framework to state-of-the-art LLMs reveals a consistent performance gap relative to general science benchmarks, diminishing return of scaling up model sizes and reasoning, and systematic weaknesses shared across top-tier models from different providers.\nL19: Large performance variation in research scenarios leads to changing choices of the best performing model on scientific discovery projects evaluated, suggesting all current LLMs are distant to general scientific \"superintelligence\". Nevertheless, LLMs already demonstrate promise in a great variety of scientific discovery projects, including cases where constituent scenario scores are low, highlighting the role of guided exploration and serendipity in discovery.\nL20: This SDE framework offers a reproducible benchmark for discovery-relevant evaluation of LLMs and charts practical paths to advance their development toward scientific discovery.\nL21: Subjects:  | Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci); Machine Learning (cs.LG); Chemical Physics (physics.chem-ph)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite63†arXiv:2512.15567 [cs.AI]\nL23:    | (or cite64†arXiv:2512.15567v1 [cs.AI] for this version)\nL24:    | cite65†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2512.15567†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Yuanqi Du [cite66†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Wed, 17 Dec 2025 16:20:03 UTC (12,988 KB)\nL29: cite5†[v2] Fri, 8 May 2026 01:14:00 UTC (13,159 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled Evaluating Large Language Models in Scientific Discovery, by Zhangde Song and 55 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite61†View PDF L38:   * cite62†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite67†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite68†view license L42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.AI\nL45: \nL46: cite69†< prev |   cite70†next > L47: \nL48: cite71†new | cite72†recent | cite73†2025-12 L49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: cite74†cond-mat L53: cite75†cond-mat.mtrl-sci L54: cite76†cs L55: cite77†cs.LG L56: cite78†physics L57: cite79†physics.chem-ph L58: \nL59: ### References & Citations\nL60: \nL61:   * cite80†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L62:   * cite81†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L63:   * cite82†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L64: \nL65: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL66: \nL67: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL68: \nL69: [Button: ×]\nL70: \nL71: loading...\nL72: \nL73: Data provided by:\nL74: ### Bookmark\nL75: \nL76: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL77: \nL78: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL79: \nL80: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL81: \nL82: Bibliographic Explorer (cite83†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL83: \nL84: [